WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.480
Grocery costs now stress 53% of Americans, opening a window for Canadian investors to screen TSX consumer staples names with stable cash flows before any rotation into defensives accelerates. This is Modern Investing Techniques, episode 138. Quick reminder, everything we discuss here is for education and entertainment. The practice investment of the day uses simulated trades with no real money.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:56.740
I'm not a licensed financial advisor and this isn't financial advice. Always do your own research before putting real money to work. The S&amp;P 500 closed at 7,786, down 2.10% of 1%. The NASDAQ composite finished at 26,729, down 3.10% of 1% and today's briefing does not carry a year-to-date move for that index, so I will not invent one.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:19.940
The TSX composite slipped to 36,730, down 1.10% of 1%. Fresh fiscal data showed the United States 2026 deficit already at $1,799 billion after 10 months, which already exceeds the entire $1,775 billion gap recorded for fiscal 2025.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:29.040
Symmetric and core inflation gauges painted mixed disinflation progress, so the next Federal Reserve move stays data-dependent.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:45.660
One filtered measure still shows progress, another looks stalled against the 2% target. The Bank of Canada sits on hold, with markets pricing a steady path into fall, and that leaves the open question of timing and size on both sides of the border still unanswered.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:58.360
Consumer facing sectors absorb the bulk of Friday's pressure as slowing signals mounted, which is exactly the tape you would expect when grocery stress is this high and defensives have not yet caught a bid.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:12.900
About 21 days ago, we picked EPD on dividend compounding momentum into the second half. The position closed down 1.45%, and underscored how quickly macro headlines can override sector tailwinds.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:24.840
For your portfolio today, that mix argues for patients on rate-sensitive duration and a tighter filter on any new entry, not a scramble into staples just because the headline looks defensive.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:34.020
Most retail traders buy call options the way they buy lottery tickets. They pick a direction, skip the rest, and then wonder why the book bleeds.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:41.960
Long call buying only has a shot when three conditions line up at the same time. Direction, magnitude, and timing.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:52.080
Miss any one of those three and the expected value of an unfiltered long call turns negative, which is exactly what the options play analysis states in plain language.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:58.300
Buying every call with no process produces losses because the raw expected value is negative.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:13.320
Today's tame consumer price index print and those mixed federal reserve inflation gauges create the kind of low drama tape where a process can still work, because you are not fighting a volatility spike that reprices every premium overnight.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:23.460
The filter is specific. Only look at names already printing volume above their 20-day average, and only if a clear catalyst sits inside the next two weeks.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:30.820
Then open your broker's options chain and compare implied volatility rank against the stock's 30-day historical move.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:37.800
Enter only when implied volatility rank sits below 40, and the stock is trading above its 50-day moving average.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:45.440
That combination historically delivered its best results in low volatility regimes when sector rotation was already underway.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:55.760
It collapses when geopolitical headlines spike the volatility index without a matching earnings revision because you end up paying rich premiums for a move that never arrives.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:02.540
If you want to try this inside a tax-free savings account, or TFSA, do not start with size.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:11.320
Paper trade one call spread per week on a single liquid name, and do not scale until three consecutive trades close above the entry delta target.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:20.200
Think of those three conditions like booking a flight. You need the destination, the distance, and the departure date confirmed before you pay the fare.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:29.600
Direction without magnitude is a shrug. Magnitude without timing is a hope. Timing without either is just decay wearing a costume.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:39.260
The process is hard on purpose. Hitting direction, magnitude, and timing together is a rare alignment, and that rarity is the point of the checklist.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:46.020
Today's simulated book is not adding a new name, and that is the lesson, not a gap in the show.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:59.620
We are monitoring LUN.TO, London Mining on the TSX, in the precious metals group after the company reaffirmed its 2026 production targets and lifted its buyback authorization.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:07.820
That pairing has historically supported multiples when copper demand stays steady, which is a real fundamental catalyst on the page.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:19.960
The technicals have not confirmed it. The stock sits 6.2% below the prior close, and is testing its 20-day moving average on daily volume that has not yet exceeded the 20-day average.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:28.940
Nearest support sits near the 50-day moving average, and a break of that line on rising volume could extend the move another 4-6%.

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:37.460
The mental stop for this simulated watch is that 50-day line, which we would treat as a maximum 5% simulated loss if we were in.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:46.560
The target, if volume ever confirms, is a retest of the prior week's high, which would imply a plus 4-6% from current levels.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:55.260
Confidence is low. Single catalyst, no volume confirmation, exactly the setup the rules require us to watch rather than enter.

00:05:56.140 --> 00:06:03.720
Yesterday's LNTH hold taught that in the most expensive way a practice book can teach it. A merger headline is not an entry.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:14.060
A merger headline plus a volume spike above the 20-day average is an entry. That is why position size on lun.to stays at zero today.

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:25.020
The buyback and the guidance are interesting. They are not complete. You see a name drop 6.2% after a buyback authorization, with output guidance still intact.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:32.180
Your simulated stop lives at the 50-day average. Do you buy the dip or do you wait for the close and the volume?

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:43.640
I wait. Headline reactions overshoot even when the fundamentals are fine, and the 6.2% drop after that announcement is the picture of an overshoot, not a completed setup.

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:48.780
This is a simulated weekly hold watch for learning purposes, not a live order.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:56.440
What you should watch into Monday is simple. A close back above the 20-day average on volume that clears the 20-day mean.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:02.340
Until those two prints appear, the checklist stays red, and the simulated capital stays in cash.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:13.420
We simulated buying lnth at Monday's open at $102.65 on the announced merger with Curium and the scale story in radio pharmaceuticals.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:27.880
We exited at Thursday's close at $101.07 after three calendar days of market data. That is a loss of 1.54%, or $15.44 on a $1,000 simulated position.

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:33.400
The thesis was catalyst-driven, and announced deal was supposed to attract follow-through.

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:38.620
Broder market volatility rose instead, and the merger news did not sustain the bid.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:46.320
The trade also produced negative alpha versus the NASDAQ over that same holding window, even though the index itself moved lower.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:50.160
A down tape does not give you a free pass if you still lagged it.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:59.880
What this teaches is blunt, announced deals still need volume confirmation before entry, especially in healthcare launches where the headline is louder than the tape.

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:08.800
Rule. Require both a fundamental catalyst and a volume spike above the 20-day average before committing capital to healthcare launches.

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:18.020
That is the same rule sitting on lun.to this morning. We are finally using it before the fill, not after the post-mortem.

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:27.480
If the long-call checklist from earlier interested you, interactive broker's trader workstation scanner is how you actually run it without a spreadsheet and a prayer.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:41.020
The scanner lets you filter options changed by implied volatility rank, volume, and earnings date proximity in a single view, which is the same pre-trade checklist institutions use, just without the sales coverage.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:50.520
Set a custom column for 20-day average volume and only surface names that are already above that threshold so you are not even looking at quiet chains.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:09:00.220
Layer in a 50-day moving average filter so that only stocks trading above that line appear, which lines up directly with the call-buying rule we just walk through.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:07.140
The same layout will show you whether implied volatility rank sits below 40 before any order ticket is even built.

